                "Notice the power of this quantitative framing: You demonstrate that you evaluate software through total lifecycle economics, not just raw execution speed. You prove that you understand operational maintenance, team cognitive load, and cloud infrastructure expenses. This total cost of ownership (TCO) perspective is the golden signature of a true Staff or Principal Systems Architect.",
                "Incorporate the concept of Reversible vs Irreversible Decisions (Jeff Bezos Type 1 vs Type 2 decisions) into your alternatives analysis. If choosing an alternative is easily reversible (like testing a new serialization library in one service), you can move fast with lightweight experimentation. But if a decision is a one-way door (like sharding a multi-terabyte production database across twenty clusters), you must apply exhaustive architectural scrutiny. Explicitly identifying decision reversibility reassures reviewers that you understand risk calibration.",
